{"id": "c057a6d1-10bf-470e-b578-478f5faef9bb", "timestamp": "2026-11-11T07:04:54.630459", "source": "user", "llm_message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "Starting this session with file context.\n\nFile path: /home/runner/work/exagium/exagium/src/router/bench/tasks/T2.md\n\nFile contents:\n--------------------\n# Task: an orphaned attestation must publish the entries it hid\n\nYou are working in `record/` of this repository (`record/record.py` and its tests in `record/test_record.py`).\n\n**Background:** the ledger writer attests ranges of entries with a predicate `{\"from_seq\", \"to_seq\", \"prev_sha256\", \"head_sha256\"}`. If a run attests a range and then fails, an \"orphan\" record explains that attestation. A reviewer found that an orphan explained only by its statement could hide an attested entry, such as a failing verdict.\n\n**Required:** add a function to `record/record.py`:\n\n    _orphan_entries_problem(orphan: dict, pred: dict) -> str | None\n\n- `orphan[\"body\"][\"entries_b64\"]` is a list of base64-encoded canonical entry bytes (as produced by `R.canonical(entry)`).\n- Return `None` when the decoded entries are exactly the attested range: there are `to_seq - from_seq + 1` of them, the first chains from `pred[\"prev_sha256\"]` (its `prev` field), each next one chains from the previous one's sha256, and the sha256 of the last one's canonical bytes equals `pred[\"head_sha256\"]`.\n- Otherwise return a short problem string:\n  - one containing the word `range` when the count is wrong;\n  - one containing `chain` when an entry's `prev` doesn't link;\n  - one containing `head` when the last hash doesn't match `head_sha256`.\n- Wire it into wherever orphans are checked, so an orphan without valid hidden entries is reported.\n- All existing tests in `record/test_record.py` must still pass.\n\nRun the tests with `cd record && python3 -m unittest test_record`.\n\n--------------------\n"}], "tool_calls": null, "tool_call_id": null, "name": null, "reasoning_content": null, "thinking_blocks": [], "responses_reasoning_item": null}, "llm_response_id": null, "activated_skills": ["simplified-technical-english"], "extended_content": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "<EXTRA_INFO>\nThe following information has been included based on a keyword match for \"STE\".\nIt may or may not be relevant to the user's request.\n\nSkill location: /home/runner/.openhands/cache/skills/public-skills/skills/simplified-technical-english/SKILL.md\n(Use this path to resolve relative file references in the skill content below)\n\n\n# Simplified Technical English\n\nASD-STE100 Simplified Technical English is a controlled-language standard from\naerospace and defense. It exists to make technical text easy to read the first\ntime, especially for people who read English as a second language. This skill\nadapts its spirit for agent prose.\n\nApply it to your **prose**: chat replies, explanations, summaries, step lists,\nrecommendations. The goal is easy reading, not dumbed-down thinking. Open the\ntext up and keep the substance.\n\n## Core rules\n\n- **Prefer short sentences.** Aim for 20 words or fewer, particularly for instructions.\n- **One idea per sentence, one topic per paragraph.** Split a sentence that carries two ideas.\n- **Active voice.** Write \"Run the tests\", not \"The tests should be run\".\n- **Plain, consistent words.** Prefer a common word over a fancy one. Use one word for one idea. Do not switch synonyms for the same thing mid-text.\n- **Say the thing directly.** Lead with the answer. Give the number, name, or path. Cut filler like \"in terms of\", \"a range of\", \"going forward\".\n- **Positive instructions.** Tell the reader what to do, not only what to avoid.\n\n## The hard boundary: prose, not code\n\nA controlled *English* vocabulary\nmust never bleed into the code the agent writes.\n\n- **Do not** simplify or rename variables, functions, types, files, config keys, CLI flags, API fields, or error strings to obey \"approved words\". Code needs exact, domain-precise names.\n- **Do not** apply it to commit messages, code comments, or reference/API documentation, where technical precision and established terminology win.\n- **Do** keep using the project's real domain terms in prose. Naming a concept correctly beats naming it \"simply\".\n\nIf clarity and a required technical term ever conflict in prose, keep the term\nand add a short plain-English gloss beside it.\n\n## When to use it\n\n- The user asked for plain or simple or clear text.\n- Instructions where a misread is costly (ops, deploys, security steps).\n- Long or dense explanations that need to be scannable.\n\n## When not to use it\n\n- Code, identifiers, commit messages, or API/reference docs (see the boundary above).\n- Creative or personal-voice writing, where rhythm and personality matter more than the controlled register.\n- Cases where a precise domain term must stay exact. Keep the term.\n\n## Attribution\n\nStandard: ASD-STE100 (ASD, the AeroSpace and Defence Industries Association of\nEurope). This skill adapts its principles for agent prose; it is not the\nofficial specification and does not reproduce its controlled dictionary.\n</EXTRA_INFO>"}], "sender": null, "critic_result": null, "kind": "MessageEvent"}
